      "citation_relevance": 0.6666666666666666,
      "answer_text": "The static table has a predefined list of field lines, each with a fixed index over time, and its entries are defined in Appendix A. The dynamic table, on the other hand, is a list of field lines maintained in first-in, first-out order, and its entries can be added or removed dynamically.\n\nThe static table's entries are identified by a unique index, and all entries in the static table have a name and a value, with values being able to be empty. The dynamic table's entries, however, are identified by an absolute index, which is a unique index for each entry in the dynamic table, and are relative to a base.\n\nThe static table's entries persist over time, whereas the dynamic table's entries can be evicted when the dynamic table capacity is reduced. \n\nRFC 9204 \u00a73.1, RFC 9204 \u00a73.2, RFC 9204 \u00a72.1"
    },
